course: dictionary
lesson: 14851
title: A name in the story
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are names, and a name often starts with a capital letter, even in the middle of a sentence. «Randy kicked the ball over the fence.» Randy is a person, because the sentence tells us he kicked a ball.
explain: Randy is a boy's or man's name, often short for Randall or Randolph. In maths problems, Randy is usually the one who owns, buys or gives things. Read the sentence and ask: what is Randy doing with the things?
explain: Randy is a common first name in the USA and Canada. A name tells you who someone is, so look at the words around it to find out what that person does.
fact: Randy is a boy's or man's name.
fact: Randy is often short for Randall or Randolph.
fact: Randy is a common first name in the USA and Canada.
fact: A name starts with a capital letter, like «Randy».
fact: «Randy bought three apples and gave one to his sister.» Here Randy is the person who buys and gives.
exercise: «Randy has five red balloons.» Who is Randy in this sentence? | A red balloon | A place in the USA | *A boy or man who owns the balloons | A kind of party food
exercise: «Randy gave two cookies to Mia.» What is Randy doing in this sentence? | *He is giving cookies away | He is eating the cookies | He is baking the cookies | He is hiding under the table
exercise: Which word in «Mum said hello to Randy at the park» is a name? | park | said | hello | *Randy
test: «Randy shared his crisps with three friends.» Is Randy a person or a thing? | A thing to eat | *A person | A place | A colour
test: Randy is often short for which longer names? | Randy or Rita | *Randall or Randolph | Ronald or Richard | Rachel or Ruth
exam: Randy has 10 pens and gives 4 to his friend. Who owns the pens at the start? | Nobody | *Randy | His friend | The pens
